Z||000009||Slammed Fiero||06-01-2001||12:34 AM||||thought you wanted a Clarion Auto PC , No wait it was a turbo , no wait rims , no lowering ....haha Just buggin you ..good luck with it all , sounds like there will be milesof wire in it all<P>Watch those fuses<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">     <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals..Autocrossed , Tracked , abused.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Nashco||06-01-2001||01:12 AM||||I'm in AL right now Joe.  I'll be in Flint in mid-July.  I go to Kettering University up there half the year.<P>Oh, and as far as wiring, slammed, I know it seems like there's a ton, but that's all part of the planning.  If you plan well, you can use integrated wiring loom (imagine the thin wiring in your p.c. but larger guage wire).  You can roll it like a fruit loops, lay it flay, bend it around corners, etc. to get it to where you need it.  Even if there's too much to plan out, you could do a hack wiring job to get everything working, then rewire it pretty and safely.<P>Jay, as far as doing everything else, I agree, never do it while driving, that pisses me off.  You need to look at it this way:  with the ability to constantly play MP3s, you can just hit play before you start driving and don't have to take your eyes off the road to change CDs.  I have an Aiwa cd player with an input that I run my laptop to currently.  On long trips (between AL, MI, and WA) I just hit play, and I have 900 songs play at random.  No need to worry about changing the CD in traffic, at night, when there's a cop around, etc <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>My brain had a thought for a second about the cameras.  That would be cool, but a little harsh for standard cameras.  Lots of RVs use back-up cameras, perhaps you should look into that.  You could recess (french) one into each side where the rear reflectors are on the bumper cover.  You could have a manually adjustable rotating mount good for a few reasons.  You could rotate them towards the outermost viewable area for normal driving and have a display on the monitor; this would take care of that hard to see area (blind spot) that the GT has built in (this would also make it more comfortable having tinted rear quarter windows).  When you want to film video, you can remove one rear camera (make sure to use good pigtails on these wires) and install it onto the front (where you'll have a set of leads that are spliced into the wires that go to the rear, good pigtails, and a mount perhaps in the front running light area.  The running light area would be good because you could hide a mount from view in the top of it well, and you don't need your blinkers while you're racing).  If you think this is a good idea, I'd be willing to dedicate an afternoon of research to help think up a good mounting method and camera choice.<P>So, you would have three permanent mounts, or just two temporary mounts if you could find some good mounting methods.  I haven't ever fooled around in either of the areas discussed, so I don't know what is there to bolt/clamp to.<P>Just a thought...I could go into more detail, but I won't.  Actually, my body is telling me it's time to go pass out..I sure hope it decides to wake up in time for work tomorrow <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||FLASHY||06-01-2001||01:37 AM||||That sounds really cool! Yet another thing I need...ahem..want...for my fiero.<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/flashy_sig.gif"> <P>"I know you believe you understand what you think I wrote, but I'm not sure you realize that what you read is not what I really meant"||||reg||14
Z||000012||onfire||06-01-2001||06:10 AM||||Here are a few websites for you. <A HREF="http://www.site-shop.com/~mitcht/in_car_computer.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.site-shop.com/~mitcht/in_car_computer.htm</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.hjnetworks.com/car/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.hjnetworks.com/car/</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://home.123mail.dk/~alm/" TARGET=_blank>http://home.123mail.dk/~alm/</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://justbecause.idleplay.net/" TARGET=_blank>http://justbecause.idleplay.net/</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||grinthock||06-01-2001||07:22 AM||||Slammed:  Yep I am not going the Clarion route, too expensive, and not enough functionality, got that fuse issues solved, bad rocker switch.  I know I can't decide what to do first, but I am gonna do this!<P>As for the wire everywhere, NOPE!  This unit is going to be FULLY CONTAINED in one unit.  The only output's will be out to the amp, camera, gps receiver and screen.<P>As for the blind spot cam's thats a cool idea...  HMMMM...  I could wire it so when the turn signal is on the system automatically changes to that cam (That's gonna require MORE software and a PLC)<P>The beauty of my design is once I have it in, it's DONE and I can add things on.  YES it's based on a PC, but not a normal PC, it's an industrial grade PC build for this use.||||reg||1
