Z||000021||OverDrive||06-22-2001||10:34 PM||||I'm an electrical engineer, and a software engineer.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> your speaking my language 100% to get it voice activated you may need to put a LOT of work, all most too much. But that aside, it's my recomendation that you build this on the Linux OS. DO NOT use windows, it'll be a huge pain in the ass. With linux, although it'll be more coding, you can have EXACTLY what you want. I've found when building new hardware, and interfacing it with a computer, linux is the best. I'd go into why, but that would make this reply about 5 pages long.<BR>This is a huge project, I dont want to sound pestimistic, but to do all your saying, will take A LOT of work, (be sure to buy lots of Tylenol). Good Luck with it though, I hope it turns out for you.<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000022||AusFiero||06-23-2001||03:41 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by grinthock:<BR><B>Everything is going smoothly but the radio will NOT be screen based, I have to remove that feature -- Oh well</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Couldnt you use a pc based radio card? Similar to the TV cards.<P>Just a thought.<P><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero5.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>Need a Subwoofer Console or Speaker Door Pods?||||reg||1
Z||000023||Pontiaddict||06-24-2001||03:09 AM||||If you want to keep your radio, try putting the screen where the heater controls are and controling them through your computer system. If you did this, you could have removed the radio too and used a larger screen. But since you already bought the screen and don't want to integrate the radio...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000024||grinthock||06-24-2001||10:29 AM||||Well Actually I have a PC Based radio card, but ALL PC BASED RADIO CARDS ONLY DO FM!  I use AM alot here, I have an AM/FM Radio Card here too but it's an ISA card and I can't find a box that has an ISA slot (and the other stuff I need, so it's NO ISA slot and use the old radio OR go with all sorts of other stuff gone!)||||reg||1
Z||000025||Oreif||08-22-2001||12:42 AM||||For the radio, Why not use an aftermarket radio but use the computer to control the IR remote. Many after market units come with a small remote for the radio/CD functions. You just need to mount an IR diode that the computer could control. You could use a programmable set-up so if you ever change the head unit just re-program the codes.<P>Just a thought.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">    <BR><B><I>Got Emblems?</I></B>||||reg||4
Z||000026||Joe Torma||08-22-2001||01:10 AM||||Grint,<P>I've already got "your" setup in my car, well almost.<P>PIII450 system<BR>Delorme GPS<BR>10.4" Touchscreen monitor<BR>Electromotive TEC II, displays all engine functions on screen, reprogammable when driving<BR>Wireless Keyboard<BR>During the winter, make all switches operate from touchscreen/voice control<BR>DVD<BR>CD<BR>10.4GIG HD<BR>Dolby 5.1 surround<BR>Voice control<BR>Video capture<BR>TV<BR>Mobile Internet(maybe even a JoesCarCam via web?!)<P>This system is intergrated into the car which includes a new dash(almost done).<P>What a pain<P>Joe<p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 08-22-2001).]||||reg||1
